# 4+1 Sector Decomposition

## Summary

Five generators yield four primitive sectors (α→D, π→A, γ→B, η→C) plus one coupling sector (ω). Char(L) = D-sector ⊔ A-sector ⊔ B-sector ⊔ C-sector ⊔ ω-sector. Induced by Langlands₀, not postulated.

## Statement

```latex
%
\label{def:four-plus-one-decomposition}
The \emph{$4{+}1$ sector decomposition}
of the boundary character space is the partition
\[
    \Char(\Lemniscate) \setminus \{0\}
    \;=\;
    \underbrace{
        \Sector{D}
        \;\sqcup\;
        \Sector{A}
        \;\sqcup\;
        \Sector{B}
        \;\sqcup\;
        \Sector{C}
    }_{\text{four primitive sectors}}
    \;\sqcup\;
    \underbrace{
        \Sector{\omega}
    }_{\text{one coupling sector}}\,.
\]
The four primitive sectors are generated
by the four primitive generators
$\alpha$, $\pi$, $\gamma$, $\eta$
via their dominant ABCD coordinates.
The coupling sector is generated
by the crossing generator~$\omega$
via its simultaneous $B$-$C$ mixing.
The count is $4 + 1$, not~$5$:
the $\omega$-sector is structurally distinct
from the primitive sectors.
```

